    "turn_text": "you know let's look at me today is called keep families together and enforce the law act and this is uh... you know i think an end up being the core of what i hope will be a bipartisan bill The other bill you're talking about the Feinstein bill, I did look at that one's got, and I'll be honest with you, it really doesn't solve the problem, and it's way too broad. This one actually solves the problem, and we can go in detail on that, but what the Feinstein bill said was, any place within 100 miles of the border, and by the way, when you include ports of entry, which you did, that includes apparently like two-thirds of our population. that there would be no detention. There's a presumption against detention. And basically, it would be the same policy that was in place in the Obama years where you have catch and release, where you stop somebody from border, then you release them pending a court case. And that just doesn't protect our border. It's a policy that's been proven not to work. So my hope is we'll be able to work together on this to say, yes, let's keep families together. And I've been outspoken on that, as you know. But we do need to have a system where somebody comes here illegally. There is some consequence. Otherwise, we're just going to have more and more people coming across the border without going through the legal process. We didn't fare for the people who are waiting in line for years and years sometimes from countries like Mexico or the Philippines when they want to come into America legally.",
